{"kind":"report","text":"approved: Architecture — affected layers are Application host binding (internal/apphost) and the workroom application under Application profile and interpreter (internal/app); this exact head preserves their contracts: docs/reference/architecture.md documents neither package's persistence mechanics, so no page update is owed there, and SaveConfig's corrected creation-only claim lives in its own doc comment where the change happened. Security — the exclusive flock on .config.lock beside ConfigFile (mode 0600, kernel-released if the process dies) spans the whole load-mutate-validate-store inside apphost.UpdateConfig; non-unix builds refuse loudly instead of racing an unprotected update; RetireActor withdraws custody through updateConfig before removing the key file, and a failed update leaves both file and memory unchanged; the mutation-checked two-process regression proves a stale Actors map cannot erase newer custody. No unresolved defect. Simplification — one generic UpdateConfig with small per-site merge rules (AddActor refuses different custody for a held name; frontier keeps the deeper depth and refuses two heads at one depth) is already the minimal shape; nothing to cut. Reproduced by me at this exact checkout: go test ./internal/app/ -run TestStaleWorkspaceSavePreservesConcurrentCustody -count=1 → ok; go test ./internal/apphost/ -run TestUpdateConfig -count=1 → ok; gofmt -l internal/app internal/apphost → clean. Accepted from the gates the two artifacts state they ran: go vet ./..., go test ./... -count=1 (disclosed flaky cmd/gitseq-mcp case needing a live resident service), -race runs, git diff --check, GOOS=windows go build.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a487901f2220b37e5d4aa261a34420e969b26803","head":"6b415ccfaea23ea88ec0073a191ef97d2ac5e78f","verdict":"approved"}}